WEBVTT

00:00.550 --> 00:02.330
So I'm curious as
to what happens

00:02.330 --> 00:04.380
if I were to take
a rational number

00:04.380 --> 00:06.870
and I were to add it to
an irrational number.

00:09.870 --> 00:16.010
Is the resulting number going
to be rational or irrational?

00:16.010 --> 00:17.650
Well, to think about
this, let's just

00:17.650 --> 00:20.520
assume it's going to be
rational and then see

00:20.520 --> 00:24.040
if this leads to any
form of contradiction.

00:24.040 --> 00:25.750
So let's assume
that this is going

00:25.750 --> 00:30.850
to give us a rational number.

00:30.850 --> 00:32.790
So let's say that this
first rational number

00:32.790 --> 00:38.860
we can represent as the ratio
of two integers, a and b.

00:38.860 --> 00:42.720
Let's call this irrational
number, let's just call this x.

00:42.720 --> 00:46.890
And their sum gives us
another rational number.

00:46.890 --> 00:49.800
Well, let's express
that as the ratio of two

00:49.800 --> 00:53.070
other integers, m and n.

00:53.070 --> 01:00.990
So we're saying that a/b
plus x is equal to m/n.

01:03.674 --> 01:05.340
Well, another way of
thinking about it--

01:05.340 --> 01:07.800
we could subtract
a/b from both sides

01:07.800 --> 01:09.670
and we would get our
irrational number

01:09.670 --> 01:18.770
x is equal to m/n
minus a/b, which

01:18.770 --> 01:22.295
is the same thing as n
times b in the denominator.

01:26.210 --> 01:27.390
And then let's see.

01:27.390 --> 01:30.835
m/n is the same
thing as mb over nb.

01:30.835 --> 01:33.330
So this would be mb.

01:33.330 --> 01:34.990
I'm just adding
these two fractions.

01:34.990 --> 01:40.850
mb minus--

01:40.850 --> 01:45.580
Let's see. a/b is the same thing
as n times a over n times b.

01:45.580 --> 01:47.470
So minus n times a.

01:52.130 --> 01:54.020
All I did is I added
these two fractions.

01:54.020 --> 01:55.850
I found a common denominator.

01:55.850 --> 01:59.170
So to make it clear, I
multiplied this one b and b,

01:59.170 --> 02:02.190
and then I multiplied
this one n and n then

02:02.190 --> 02:05.250
I just added these two things,
and I got this expression right

02:05.250 --> 02:06.480
over here.

02:06.480 --> 02:08.909
So this denominator
is clearly an integer.

02:08.909 --> 02:10.630
I have the product
of two integers.

02:10.630 --> 02:12.380
That's going to be an integer.

02:12.380 --> 02:14.690
That's going to be an integer.

02:14.690 --> 02:17.520
And then this numerator,
mb, is an integer.

02:17.520 --> 02:18.509
na is an integer.

02:18.509 --> 02:19.800
The difference of two integers.

02:19.800 --> 02:21.591
This whole thing is
going to be an integer.

02:24.740 --> 02:29.290
So it looks like, assuming
that the sum is rational,

02:29.290 --> 02:31.290
that all of a sudden we
have this contradiction.

02:31.290 --> 02:35.380
We assumed that x is irrational,
we're assuming x is irrational,

02:35.380 --> 02:37.706
but, all of a sudden, because
we made that assumption,

02:37.706 --> 02:39.580
we're able to assume
that we can represent it

02:39.580 --> 02:42.510
as this ratio of two integers.

02:42.510 --> 02:45.265
So this tells us that
x must be rational.

02:48.720 --> 02:51.480
And that is the contradiction.

02:51.480 --> 02:54.450
That is a very large
contradiction right over there.

02:54.450 --> 02:57.140
The assumption was
that x is irrational.

02:57.140 --> 03:00.090
Now we got that x
must be rational.

03:00.090 --> 03:02.920
So, therefore, this
cannot be the case.

03:02.920 --> 03:05.980
A rational plus an
irrational must--

03:05.980 --> 03:08.460
so this is not right--
a rational plus

03:08.460 --> 03:10.530
an irrational must
be irrational.

03:10.530 --> 03:11.670
Let me write that down.

03:11.670 --> 03:22.870
So a rational plus an
irrational must be irrational.
